var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/formatting/#Formatting","page":"Formatting","text":"ODSFiles formats cells with XLSX.jl's functions: setFont, setFill, setBorder, setAlignment and setFormat, with their getters and uniform variants. They take XLSX.jl's address forms: a cell (\"B2\"), a range (\"B2:D4\"), whole columns (\"A:C\") or rows (\"3:5\"), a list of ranges (\"A1:B2,D4\"), a defined name, a sheet-qualified reference on the file (xf[\"Sheet1.A1\"] style: setFont(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\"; …)), or rows and columns as integers, ranges, vectors or :.\n\nusing ODSFiles\n\nxf = newods()\ns = xf[1]\ns[\"A1\"] = \"Total\"\ns[\"B1\"] = 1234.5\n\nsetFont(s, \"A1\"; bold = true, size = 12, color = \"darkblue\")\nsetFill(s, \"A1:B1\"; pattern = \"solid\", fgColor = \"FFF2CC\")\nsetBorder(s, \"A1:B1\"; outside = [\"style\" => \"medium\"])\nsetAlignment(s, \"B1\"; horizontal = \"right\")\nsetFormat(s, \"B1\"; format = \"#,##0.00\")\n\nODSFiles.getText(s, \"B1\")      # \"1,234.50\"\nwriteods(\"report.ods\", xf)","title":"Formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formatting/#Merged-cells","page":"Formatting","text":"mergeCells(s, \"A1:C1\")                 # A1 keeps its value; B1 and C1 are emptied and covered\ngetMergedCells(s)                      # [A1:C1], or nothing\nisMergedCell(s, \"B1\")                  # true\ngetMergedBaseCell(s, \"B1\")             # (baseCell = A1, baseValue = …)\nremoveMergedCells(s, \"A1:C1\")          # or removeMergedCells(s) for every merge\n\nA merge is an anchor cell and the cells it covers, as LibreOffice writes it. Covered cells keep their formatting. An explicit range may reach past the used area.","title":"Merged cells"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formatting/#Column-widths,-row-heights-and-autofit","page":"Formatting","text":"setColumnWidth(s, \"A:B\"; width = 20)           # in characters, as in XLSX.jl\nsetColumnWidth(s, \"C\"; width = 3, unit = :cm)  # or :mm, :in, :pt\nODSFiles.getColumnWidth(s, \"A1\")               # 20.7109375, as XLSX.jl reports it\nsetRowHeight(s, 1; height = 30)                # in points\nautofitColumns(s)                              # LibreOffice's optimal width for every column\n\nwritetable(\"out.ods\", df; column_widths = [12, 30], column_formats = Dict(\"price\" => \"#,##0.00\"))\n\nODF widths are lengths. A character is the width LibreOffice gives one when it opens an XLSX.jl file, so a width looks the same in both. autofitColumns measures each cell's text as LibreOffice shows it, in the cell's font. column_widths and column_formats also work when streaming.","title":"Column widths, row heights and autofit"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formatting/#Named-cell-styles","page":"Formatting","text":"ODF documents have named cell styles, the ones LibreOffice shows in its Styles deck: \"Default\", \"Heading 1\", \"Good\", and styles of your own. XLSX.jl has nothing like them.\n\nsetCellStyle applies a style.\naddCellStyle! defines one, from the same keywords the setters take.\ngetCellStyle gives a cell's style.\n\naddCellStyle!(xf, \"Total\"; parent = \"Default\",\n              font = (bold = true, size = 12), fill = (pattern = \"solid\", fgColor = \"FFF2CC\"),\n              border = (top = [\"style\" => \"double\"],), format = \"#,##0.00\")\nsetCellStyle(s, \"A10:B10\", \"Total\")\nsetCellStyle(s, \"A1\", \"Heading 1\")      # one of LibreOffice's built-in styles\nODSFiles.getCellStyle(s, \"A1\")         # \"Heading 1\"\n\nDirect formatting stays on top, as in LibreOffice. Applying a style removes the cell's own settings only for what the style sets (its font size, say). The rest stays, including the number format unless the style has one.\nLibreOffice's built-in styles are added, in LibreOffice's own definitions, to a file that lacks them: Heading, Heading 1, Heading 2, Text, Note, Footnote, Hyperlink, Status, Good, Neutral, Bad, Warning, Error, Accent, Accent 1 to 3, and Result.\nNames are the ones LibreOffice shows. In the file, a name such as \"My Style\" is stored as My_20_Style; either form is accepted.","title":"Named cell styles"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formatting/#How-it-differs-from-XLSX.jl","page":"Formatting","text":"Empty cells are formatted, as in LibreOffice. Whole columns and rows are formatted to the end of the sheet, not only up to the used area.\nStyles, not tables. ODF has no font, fill or border tables: a cell has a style, which ODSFiles derives from the cell's current one (named styles stay its parent). Setters return the new style's name for a single cell, and getters report the properties the cell's style resolves to, also on files opened read-only.\nWhat ODF can't hold: superscript and subscript (vertAlign) are refused; pattern fills are blended into one colour, as LibreOffice does when it opens an xlsx file.\nNumber formats are stored as ODF data styles, in the form LibreOffice writes for the same code. getText shows a value as LibreOffice shows it, which differs from Excel in places (General shows up to 15 significant digits, for example).\nMerged cells are an anchor cell and covered cells, as LibreOffice writes them.\n\nThe full list is in the workspace's records/api-divergence.md.","title":"How it differs from XLSX.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Files-and-sheets","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Files and sheets"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reading-cells","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Reading cells"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Tables","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Tables"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Writing","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Writing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Rows-and-columns","page":"API reference","text":"Inserting and deleting rows and columns moves every reference to them as LibreOffice moves it: formulas on every sheet, defined names, merges, conditional formats, validations, database ranges, print ranges, charts and anchored shapes.","title":"Rows and columns"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Formatting","page":"API reference","text":"Fonts, fills, borders and alignment:\n\nNumber formats and the text a cell shows:\n\nUniform formatting:\n\nColumn widths and row heights:\n\nNamed cell styles:\n\nMerged cells:","title":"Formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-shared-core","page":"API reference","text":"The format-independent parts of ODSFiles (references, tables, number formats, dates) live in a submodule meant to be shared with XLSX.jl in future. It is not part of the public API.","title":"The shared core"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ODSFiles.ODSFiles","page":"API reference","text":"ODSFiles\n\nRead and write OpenDocument spreadsheets (.ods, .fods, .ots) in pure Julia.\n\nThe public API mirrors XLSX.jl wherever that is reasonable (see records/api-divergence.md in the workspace).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.ODSFiles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.readods","page":"API reference","text":"readods(source) -> ODSFile\n\nRead an OpenDocument spreadsheet from a file path or an IO: openods with the cache enabled. Each sheet is parsed the first time it is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.readods"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.openods","page":"API reference","text":"openods(source; mode=\"r\", enable_cache=true) -> ODSFile\nopenods(f::Function, source; mode=\"r\", enable_cache=true)\n\nOpen an OpenDocument spreadsheet (.ods, .fods or .ots, detected from the content) from a file path or an IO. The do-block form passes the file to f and returns what f returns.\n\ncontent.xml is read once, and the sheets are located without being parsed.\n\nenable_cache=true: a sheet is parsed into memory the first time one of its cells is read; later reads use that copy.\nenable_cache=false: every read scans the sheet again, keeping nothing, and stops after the last row it needs. Use it with eachrow to stream a large sheet.\n\nModes, as in XLSX.jl:\n\n\"r\": read only.\n\"rw\": read and edit; save with saveods or writeods. Saving patches the original file: everything ODSFiles does not edit is kept as it was (D-023).\n\"w\": start a new, empty spreadsheet (with one sheet, Sheet1) that will be written to source; an existing file there is not read.\n\nWriting modes need enable_cache=true. With the do-block form, a file opened for writing is saved to source after f returns (not if f throws).\n\nSee also readods, newods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.openods"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.newods","page":"API reference","text":"newods(sheetname=\"\") -> ODSFile\n\nA new, empty spreadsheet with one sheet (Sheet1, or sheetname), open for writing. Write it with writeods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.newods"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.writeods","page":"API reference","text":"writeods(dest, xf::ODSFile; overwrite=false, version=\"1.4\") -> path or nothing\n\nWrite xf to dest, a file path or an IO.\n\nThe format follows the extension of a path: .ods, .ots (a template) or .fods (flat XML). Writing to an IO keeps the format the file was read in (.ods for a new one).\nA file that was read is written by patching it (D-023): what ODSFiles does not edit is kept exactly as it was. It can be written as a template or a document, but not converted between zipped and flat XML yet.\nA new file is written in ODF 1.4, or 1.3 with version=\"1.3\" (D-006).\ndisplay_text=false leaves out the display text (text:p) of the number, date, time and boolean cells that ODSFiles writes; readers recompute it from the value. Files are then smaller (about half, for numeric data) and faster to write (D-029).\nAn existing file at dest is only replaced with overwrite=true.\n\nA path becomes the file's new source (for saveods). Returns the absolute path, or nothing for an IO.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.writeods"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.saveods","page":"API reference","text":"saveods(xf::ODSFile)\n\nSave xf back to where it was opened from: its file, or the IO it was read from (which must be writable and seekable; its content is replaced). A file made with newods has nowhere to go: use writeods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.saveods"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.ODSFile","page":"API reference","text":"ODSFile\n\nAn opened spreadsheet, as openods and readods return it. Index it by sheet name or number (xf[\"Sheet1\"], xf[1]), or with a sheet-qualified reference or a defined name (xf[\"Sheet1.A1:B2\"], xf[\"Sheet1!A1\"], xf[\"myrange\"]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.ODSFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.Worksheet","page":"API reference","text":"Worksheet\n\nA sheet of an ODSFile. Index it like a matrix of values (sheet[\"A1\"], sheet[\"A1:B3\"], sheet[2, 1], sheet[:]); see getdata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.Worksheet"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.sheetnames","page":"API reference","text":"sheetnames(xf::ODSFile) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe names of the sheets, in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.sheetnames"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.sheetcount","page":"API reference","text":"sheetcount(xf::ODSFile) -> Int\n\nThe number of sheets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.sheetcount"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.hassheet","page":"API reference","text":"hassheet(xf::ODSFile, sheetname) -> Bool\n\nWhether the file has a sheet named sheetname. Names match without regard to case, as in LibreOffice, which does not allow two sheets whose names differ only in case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.hassheet"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.addsheet!","page":"API reference","text":"addsheet!(xf::ODSFile, name=\"\") -> Worksheet\n\nAdd an empty sheet at the end. Without a name, it is called SheetN (the first free N).\n\n\n\n\n\naddsheet!(s::StreamWriter, name=\"\"; columns=nothing, column_widths=nothing, column_formats=nothing) -> StreamSheet\naddsheet!(s::StreamWriter, name, table; column_widths, column_formats) -> StreamSheet\n\nStart the next sheet of a streamed file, finishing the one before. columns writes a header row; a Tables.jl table writes its header and all its rows. column_widths and column_formats set the columns' widths and the number formats of the rows written after the header, as in writetable!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.addsheet!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.ODSError","page":"API reference","text":"ODSError\n\nThe exception ODSFiles throws for its own errors: an invalid reference, a missing sheet, a file that is not an ODF spreadsheet, and so on. It is the shared core's SheetCoreError (D-021), so one type covers errors from both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.ODSError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.getdata","page":"API reference","text":"getdata(sheet, ref)\ngetdata(sheet, row, column)\ngetdata(sheet)\n\nValues from a worksheet: a scalar for a single cell, a Matrix{Any} for a rectangular range, or a vector of matrices for a list of ranges. Empty cells, empty strings and errors read as missing.\n\nref is a cell or range (\"A1\", \"A1:C4\", \"A:B\", \"1:4\", \"A1:A4,C1\"), a sheet-qualified one in LibreOffice's or Excel's notation (\"Sheet1.A1:C4\", \"Sheet1!A1:C4\"), or a defined name. Rows and columns can also be integers, unit ranges, step ranges, vectors or :. For whole columns or rows, the other extent is the sheet's get_dimension.\n\nIndexing a Worksheet calls getdata: sheet[\"A1\"], sheet[1:3, 2], sheet[:].\n\nThe value types are described in Cell: Int64 or Float64 for numbers, String, Bool, Date or DateTime, and Time (or a CompoundPeriod for 24 hours or more).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.getdata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.readdata","page":"API reference","text":"readdata(source, sheet, ref)\nreaddata(source, sheetref)\n\nOpen source (a path or an IO) without the cache, and return getdata of ref on sheet (a name or a number), or of a sheet-qualified reference such as \"Sheet1.A1:B3\" (\"Sheet1!A1:B3\" also works).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.readdata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getcell","page":"API reference","text":"getcell(sheet, ref)\ngetcell(sheet, row, col)\n\nThe Cell at ref, or an EmptyCell where the sheet holds nothing. For a range, a Matrix{AbstractCell} (see getcellrange); ref takes the same forms as in getdata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getcell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getcellrange","page":"API reference","text":"getcellrange(sheet, rng)\n\nThe cells of a range as a Matrix{AbstractCell}, or a vector of matrices for a list of ranges. rng takes the forms of getdata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getcellrange"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.get_dimension","page":"API reference","text":"get_dimension(ws::Worksheet; allow_load=true) -> CellRange\n\nThe smallest range holding every cell with a value or formula. ODS files do not record it, so it is computed from the sheet (the cache, or a scan without the cache). An empty sheet gives A1:A1, as in XLSX.jl. With allow_load=false, an unread sheet gives A1:A1 without being read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.get_dimension"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.Cell","page":"API reference","text":"Cell\n\nA cell read from a worksheet. Fields:\n\nref::CellRef: its position\ndatatype::Symbol: the ODF value type: :float, :percentage, :currency, :date, :time, :boolean, :string, :error, or :empty (a formula with no result, or a covered cell)\nvalue: its value, as getdata returns it\nformula: the stored formula (\"of:=[.A1]*3\"), or nothing; getFormula gives it in LibreOffice's notation\nstyle::String: the name of its effective cell style\ncurrency: the currency code of a currency cell (\"GBP\"), else nothing\nerror: the displayed text of an error result (\"#DIV/0!\", \"Err:502\"), else nothing\ncolspan, rowspan: the size of a merge whose top-left cell this is (1 otherwise)\ncovered::Bool: whether the cell is hidden by a merge\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.Cell"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.EmptyCell","page":"API reference","text":"EmptyCell(ref)\n\nA position where the worksheet holds no value and no formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.EmptyCell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.iserror","page":"API reference","text":"iserror(sheet, ref)\niserror(sheet, row, col)\n\nWhether a cell holds an error result (#DIV/0!, #N/A, Err:502, ...), which getdata reads as missing. For a range, a matrix of Bools (a vector of them for a list of ranges). The ODF file marks errors with calcext:value-type=\"error\" (F-023).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.iserror"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.geterror","page":"API reference","text":"geterror(sheet, ref)\ngeterror(sheet, row, col)\n\nThe displayed text of an error result, such as \"#DIV/0!\" or \"Err:502\", or \"\" for a cell without one. For a range, a matrix of strings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.geterror"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getFormula","page":"API reference","text":"getFormula(sheet, ref; raw=false) -> Union{String, Nothing}\ngetFormula(sheet, row, col; raw=false)\ngetFormula(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\"; raw=false)\n\nThe formula of a cell, as LibreOffice shows it (\"=SUM(A1:B2,1)\", \"='My sheet'.A1*2\"), or nothing if the cell has none. With raw=true, the formula as stored in the file (\"of:=SUM([.A1:.B2];1)\").\n\nThe UI notation follows LibreOffice with its en-US settings: , between function arguments and between the columns of an inline array, ; between its rows. Formulas written in another namespace than OpenFormula (such as msoxl:) lose their prefix and are otherwise returned unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getFormula"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetRow","page":"API reference","text":"SheetRow\n\nA row of a worksheet, as eachrow gives it. row_number(r) is its number; r[col] (a column number or name such as \"B\") is a value, getcell(r, col) a cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetRow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.eachrow-Tuple{Worksheet}","page":"API reference","text":"eachrow(sheet::Worksheet)\n\nIterate the rows of sheet that have content, as SheetRows, in order. Repeated rows are given one by one. With the cache on, the sheet is read into the cache first; with enable_cache=false (openods), rows are read as they are iterated.\n\nfor r in eachrow(xf[\"Sheet1\"])\n    println(row_number(r), \": \", r[1], \", \", r[\"B\"])\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.eachrow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.row_number","page":"API reference","text":"row_number(c::CellRef) :: Int\n\nReturns the row number of a given cell reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.row_number"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.column_number","page":"API reference","text":"column_number(c::CellRef) :: Int\n\nReturns the column number of a given cell reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.column_number"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.CellRef","page":"API reference","text":"CellRef(n::AbstractString)\nCellRef(row::Int, col::Int)\n\nA CellRef represents a cell location given by row and column identifiers.\n\nCellRef(\"B6\") indicates a cell located at column 2 and row 6.\n\nThese row and column integers can also be passed directly to the CellRef constructor: CellRef(6,2) == CellRef(\"B6\").\n\nFinally, a convenience macro @ref_str is provided: ref\"B6\" == CellRef(\"B6\").\n\nExamples\n\ncn = SheetCore.CellRef(\"AB1\")\nprintln( SheetCore.row_number(cn) ) # will print 1\nprintln( SheetCore.column_number(cn) ) # will print 28\nprintln( string(cn) ) # will print out AB1\nprintln( cellname(cn) ) # will print out AB1\n\ncn = SheetCore.CellRef(1, 28)\nprintln( SheetCore.row_number(cn) ) # will print 1\nprintln( SheetCore.column_number(cn) ) # will print 28\nprintln( string(cn) ) # will print out AB1\nprintln( cellname(cn) ) # will print out AB1\n\ncn = SheetCore.ref\"AB1\"\nprintln( SheetCore.row_number(cn) ) # will print 1\nprintln( SheetCore.column_number(cn) ) # will print 28\nprintln( string(cn) ) # will print out AB1\nprintln( cellname(cn) ) # will print out AB1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.CellRef"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCellRef","page":"API reference","text":"SheetCellRef{S<:RefStyle}(ref::AbstractString)\nSheetCellRef{S<:RefStyle}(sheet::AbstractString, cellref::CellRef)\n\nA cell on a named sheet: a CellRef together with the name of its sheet, in the fields sheet and cellref. S is the notation it is parsed from and shown in (RefStyle); each back end binds SheetCellRef to its own style.\n\nSheetCellRef{ExcelStyle}(\"Sheet1!B2\") refers to cell B2 on sheet Sheet1. A sheet name containing spaces or other special characters is quoted, as in Excel: SheetCellRef{ExcelStyle}(\"'Sheet 1'!B2\"), which SheetCellRef{ExcelStyle}(\"Sheet 1\", CellRef(\"B2\")) also gives. In ODFStyle the same cell is Sheet1.B2 and 'Sheet 1'.B2.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> r = SheetCore.SheetCellRef{SheetCore.ExcelStyle}(\"Sheet1!B2\")\nSheet1!B2\n\njulia> SheetCore.SheetCellRef{SheetCore.ODFStyle}(\"'My sheet'.B2\")\n'My sheet'.B2\n\njulia> r.sheet\n\"Sheet1\"\n\njulia> r.cellref\nB2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCellRef"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.readtable","page":"API reference","text":"readtable(source, [sheet, [columns]]; [first_row], [column_labels], [header],\n          [infer_eltypes], [stop_in_empty_row], [stop_in_row_function],\n          [keep_empty_rows], [normalizenames], [missing_strings]) -> DataTable\n\nRead a table from a file path or IO without keeping the sheet in memory: gettable on sheet (a name or number; the first sheet if not given) of the file opened with enable_cache=false.\n\nUse first_row to give the first row of the table (its header, if header=true). Without it, the table starts at the first row with data in its first column.\n\nheader (default true) says whether the first row holds the column labels. Without a header, and without column_labels, the labels are the column letters.\n\ncolumn_labels gives the labels as a vector of Symbols or strings.\n\nnormalizenames=true turns the labels into valid Julia identifiers.\n\nmissing_strings gives strings (one, or a vector) to read as missing.\n\ninfer_eltypes (default true) gives each column the narrowest element type of its values; otherwise columns are Vector{Any}.\n\nstop_in_empty_row (default true) ends the table at the first empty row. stop_in_row_function is a function of a TableRow returning true at the row that ends the table (that row is left out).\n\nkeep_empty_rows (default false) keeps rows whose values are all missing between the first and the last row of the table.\n\nenable_cache is accepted for compatibility with XLSX.jl and ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.readtable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.gettable","page":"API reference","text":"gettable(sheet, [columns]; [first_row], [column_labels], [header], [infer_eltypes],\n         [stop_in_empty_row], [stop_in_row_function], [keep_empty_rows],\n         [normalizenames], [missing_strings]) -> DataTable\n\nThe table in sheet as a DataTable, which any Tables.jl sink accepts (DataFrame(gettable(sheet))). columns selects the columns (\"B:D\"); without it, the table's columns are found as in eachtablerow.\n\nUse first_row to give the first row of the table (its header, if header=true). Without it, the table starts at the first row with data in its first column.\n\nheader (default true) says whether the first row holds the column labels. Without a header, and without column_labels, the labels are the column letters.\n\ncolumn_labels gives the labels as a vector of Symbols or strings.\n\nnormalizenames=true turns the labels into valid Julia identifiers.\n\nmissing_strings gives strings (one, or a vector) to read as missing.\n\ninfer_eltypes (default true) gives each column the narrowest element type of its values; otherwise columns are Vector{Any}.\n\nstop_in_empty_row (default true) ends the table at the first empty row. stop_in_row_function is a function of a TableRow returning true at the row that ends the table (that row is left out).\n\nkeep_empty_rows (default false) keeps rows whose values are all missing between the first and the last row of the table.\n\n\n\n\n\ngettable(itr::TableRowIterator; infer_eltypes=true) -> DataTable\n\nRead the table's rows into a DataTable. With infer_eltypes, each column gets the narrowest element type of its values (plus Missing), else Any.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.gettable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.eachtablerow","page":"API reference","text":"eachtablerow(sheet, [columns]; [first_row], [column_labels], [header],\n             [stop_in_empty_row], [stop_in_row_function], [keep_empty_rows],\n             [normalizenames], [missing_strings]) -> TableRowIterator\n\nAn iterator of the rows of the table in sheet, each a TableRow (index it with a column number or label: r[1], r[:name]). columns is a column range such as \"B:E\"; without it, the columns are the first run of consecutive non-empty cells in the table's first row.\n\nUse first_row to give the first row of the table (its header, if header=true). Without it, the table starts at the first row with data in its first column.\n\nheader (default true) says whether the first row holds the column labels. Without a header, and without column_labels, the labels are the column letters.\n\ncolumn_labels gives the labels as a vector of Symbols or strings.\n\nnormalizenames=true turns the labels into valid Julia identifiers.\n\nmissing_strings gives strings (one, or a vector) to read as missing.\n\ninfer_eltypes (default true) gives each column the narrowest element type of its values; otherwise columns are Vector{Any}.\n\nstop_in_empty_row (default true) ends the table at the first empty row. stop_in_row_function is a function of a TableRow returning true at the row that ends the table (that row is left out).\n\nkeep_empty_rows (default false) keeps rows whose values are all missing between the first and the last row of the table.\n\n\n\n\n\neachtablerow(rows, [columns]; sheetname=\"\", first_row, column_labels, header=true,\n             stop_in_empty_row=true, stop_in_row_function, keep_empty_rows=false,\n             normalizenames=false, missing_strings) -> TableRowIterator\n\nThe table in a row source rows (see the row protocol in this file). The back ends wrap it for their worksheets. columns is a ColumnRange or a string such as \"B:E\"; without it, the table's columns are the first run of consecutive non-empty cells in its first row. sheetname is used in messages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.eachtablerow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.readto","page":"API reference","text":"readto(source, [sheet, [columns]], sink; kw...)\n\nRead a table with readtable and pass it to the Tables.jl sink, such as DataFrame: readto(\"data.ods\", \"Sheet1\", DataFrame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.readto"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.DataTable","page":"API reference","text":"DataTable is a simple Tables.jl compatible data structure that holds tabular data read from a spreadsheet: a vector of columns and their labels.\n\nPass a DataTable to any Tables.jl compatible sink, e.g. DataFrame(dt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.DataTable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.TableRow","page":"API reference","text":"TableRow\n\nA row of a table: row (its index in the table, not the sheet row), the table's Index, and its values. Index it with a column number or label: r[1], r[:name].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.TableRow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore.TableRowIterator","page":"API reference","text":"TableRowIterator\n\nAn iterator of TableRows over a row source, as eachtablerow builds it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore.TableRowIterator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setdata!","page":"API reference","text":"setdata!(sheet, ref, value)\nsetdata!(sheet, row, col, value)\nsheet[ref] = value\nsheet[row, col] = value\n\nWrite value into a cell, or into every cell of a range. The file must be writable (openods with mode=\"rw\" or \"w\", or newods).\n\nvalue may be a number, string, Bool, Date, DateTime, Time, a period (a duration), or missing/nothing to empty the cell.\nA matrix fills a range of its size.\nA vector fills a row (dim=2, the default) or a column (dim=1) from a cell, or a one-row or one-column range.\nref takes the forms of getdata: \"B2\", \"B2:D4\", \"Sheet1.B2\", \"Sheet1!B2\", integers and ranges.\nWith : for the rows or columns, as in XLSX.jl: ws[3, :] = [1, 2, 3] writes a vector along row 3 from column A, and ws[:, 2] = v down column B from row 1. Any other value fills the cells addressed, where : stands for the sheet's used columns or rows (ws[3, :] = 0, ws[:, 2:3] = missing, ws[:] = 1).\n\nA cell keeps its style when that style's format fits the new value. Otherwise dates, date-times, times and booleans get ISO formats, and numbers and text the Default style (D-024, D-027).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setdata!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setFormula","page":"API reference","text":"setFormula(sheet, ref; val, raw=false)\nsetFormula(sheet, row, col; val, raw=false)\n\nWrite a formula into a cell, or into every cell of a range (the same formula in each). val is in LibreOffice's notation, as getFormula gives it: \"=SUM(B1:B3,'My sheet'.A1)\". It is stored as OpenFormula (of:=SUM([.B1:.B3];['My sheet'.A1])) (D-025). With raw=true, val is stored as given, and must be a stored formula such as \"of:=[.A1]*2\".\n\nThe cell's value is left empty: LibreOffice computes it when it opens the file (F-028).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setFormula"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.writetable","page":"API reference","text":"writetable(filename, data, columnnames; overwrite=false, sheetname=\"\", anchor_cell=\"A1\", streaming=false)\nwritetable(filename, table; overwrite=false, sheetname=\"\", anchor_cell=\"A1\", streaming=false)\nwritetable(filename, \"Sheet1\" => table1, \"Sheet2\" => table2, ...; overwrite=false, streaming=false)\nwritetable(filename, [(sheetname, data, columnnames), ...]; overwrite=false, streaming=false)\nwritetable(filename; overwrite=false, streaming=false, Sheet1=(data, columnnames), ...)\n\nWrite tables to a new file (a path or an IO), one per sheet, each with a header row. The format follows the file's extension (.ods, .ots, .fods). An existing file is only replaced with overwrite=true. With streaming=true, rows are written as they are produced, in bounded memory (streamods); anchor_cell is then not available. display_text=false leaves out the display text of number, date, time and boolean cells (smaller files, faster writes; see writeods). column_widths and column_formats lay out every sheet's columns, as in writetable!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.writetable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.writetable!","page":"API reference","text":"writetable!(sheet, data, columnnames; anchor_cell=\"A1\", write_columnnames=true, column_widths, column_formats)\nwritetable!(sheet, table; anchor_cell=\"A1\", write_columnnames=true, column_widths, column_formats)\n\nWrite a table into sheet, with its column names as a header row (unless write_columnnames=false) and its top-left cell at anchor_cell. data is a vector of columns and columnnames their names; or table is any Tables.jl table.\n\ncolumn_widths: one width for every column, or a vector of widths by column (nothing leaves a column's width as it is), in characters as setColumnWidth takes them.\ncolumn_formats: a Dict from column (number from the first written, or name) to a number format, as setFormat takes it, applied to the column's data cells.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.writetable!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.streamods","page":"API reference","text":"streamods(f, dest; overwrite=false, version=\"1.4\", display_text=true, declare_widths=[])\n\nWrite a new file to dest (a path or an IO) row by row, in bounded memory. f gets a StreamWriter: add sheets with addsheet!(s, name; columns) (a header row) or addsheet!(s, name, table) (a Tables.jl table, header and rows), and write rows with writerow! and writerows!. The format follows the extension (.ods, .ots; flat .fods is not streamed). display_text=false leaves out the display text of number, date, time and boolean cells, as in writeods.\n\nColumn widths (addsheet!(…; column_widths)) are declared at the start of the file, before its first sheet: a width a later sheet uses must be the first sheet's too, or be listed in declare_widths (in characters). writetable(…; streaming=true) declares them all.\n\nstreamods(\"big.ods\") do s\n    sh = addsheet!(s, \"data\"; columns = [\"n\", \"square\"])\n    for n in 1:1_000_000\n        writerow!(sh, (n, n^2))\n    end\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.streamods"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.StreamWriter","page":"API reference","text":"StreamWriter\n\nA file being written by streamods. Add sheets to it with addsheet!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.StreamWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.StreamSheet","page":"API reference","text":"StreamSheet\n\nA sheet being written by streamods. Write rows to it in order with writerow! and writerows!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.StreamSheet"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.writerow!","page":"API reference","text":"writerow!(sh::StreamSheet, row)\n\nWrite the next row of a streamed sheet: a tuple, NamedTuple or vector of values (missing or nothing leave a cell empty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.writerow!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.writerows!","page":"API reference","text":"writerows!(sh::StreamSheet, table)\n\nWrite every row of a Tables.jl table (without its header) to a streamed sheet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.writerows!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.insertRows","page":"API reference","text":"insertRows(sheet, at; count = 1)\ninsertRows(xf, \"Sheet1.5\"; count = 1)\n\nInsert count empty rows before row at (an integer, or a string such as \"5\"). The rows from at down move down, and everything that refers to them follows, as in LibreOffice: formulas on every sheet, defined names, merged cells, conditional formats, validations, database ranges, print ranges, charts and anchored shapes. A range that the new rows fall inside, or whose last row is at at, grows (D-050).\n\nThe new rows take the cell formats of the row above, and its height when that height was set, and are not hidden (D-051). Inserting at row 1 gives unformatted rows. An insert that would push a cell with content or formatting off the sheet is an error, as it is in LibreOffice (D-052).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.insertRows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.insertColumns","page":"API reference","text":"insertColumns(sheet, at; count = 1)\ninsertColumns(xf, \"Sheet1.C\"; count = 1)\n\nInsert count empty columns before column at (an integer, or a column such as \"C\"). As insertRows, along the other axis. The new columns take the cell formats and default cell style of the column to their left, and the width of the column they push to the right (D-051).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.insertColumns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.deleteRows","page":"API reference","text":"deleteRows(sheet, rows)\ndeleteRows(xf, \"Sheet1.3:5\")\n\nDelete rows: an integer, a range, a vector of row numbers, or a string (\"3\", \"3:5\", \"3:5,9\" or \"3:5~9\"; a cell or range gives its rows, a defined name its range's rows). The rows below move up, and everything that refers to them follows, as in LibreOffice (D-050): a reference to a deleted cell becomes #REF!, and a range that loses some of its rows shrinks. Merges whose first row is deleted are undone; validations, conditional formats and annotations on deleted cells go with them.\n\nNot yet as LibreOffice does it: a shape anchored in a deleted cell is deleted with it (LibreOffice moves it to the next cell), and a validation that no cell uses any more stays in the file, unused (LibreOffice drops it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.deleteRows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.deleteColumns","page":"API reference","text":"deleteColumns(sheet, cols)\ndeleteColumns(xf, \"Sheet1.B:C\")\n\nDelete columns: an integer, a range, a vector of column numbers, or a string (\"B\", \"B:C\", \"B:C,F\"; a cell or range gives its columns, a defined name its range's columns). As deleteRows, along the other axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.deleteColumns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setFont","page":"API reference","text":"setFont(sheet, ref; bold, italic, under, strike, size, color, name) -> String or -1\nsetFont(sheet, row, col; …)\nsetFont(xf, \"Sheet1.A1:B2\"; …)\n\nSet font attributes of a cell, a range, whole columns (\"A:C\", (sheet, :, 2:4)), whole rows (\"3:5\", (sheet, 2, :)), a list of ranges (\"A1:B2,D4\") or a defined name; rows and columns can also be integers, ranges, vectors or :. Only the attributes given change.\n\nbold, italic, strike: true or false\nunder: \"single\", \"double\" or \"none\"\nsize: in points, 1 to 409\ncolor: a hex colour (\"FF0000\", \"#FF0000\", \"FFFF0000\"), a Colors.jl name or a Colorant\nname: a font name (declared as a font face in the file)\nvertAlign: only \"baseline\"; ODF cells have no superscript or subscript (D-036)\n\nAs in LibreOffice, empty cells are formatted too, and whole columns and rows are formatted as such, not only up to the used area (D-033). Returns the name of the cell's new style for a single cell, else -1 (XLSX.jl returns a font id; ODF has no font table, D-032).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setFont"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setFill","page":"API reference","text":"setFill(sheet, ref; pattern, fgColor, bgColor) -> String or -1\n\nSet the background of cells (address forms as setFont). pattern=\"solid\" with fgColor gives a solid background, \"none\" none. ODF cells have no pattern fills: other patterns blend fgColor into bgColor (white by default) as LibreOffice does when it imports one (D-036).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setFill"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setBorder","page":"API reference","text":"setBorder(sheet, ref; left, right, top, bottom, diagonal, allsides, outside) -> String or -1\n\nSet cell borders (address forms as setFont). Each side is a vector of pairs: [\"style\" => \"thin\", \"color\" => \"red\"]; diagonal also takes \"direction\" => \"up\", \"down\" or \"both\". allsides sets the four sides. outside sets the border around a range as a whole, alone. Styles are XLSX.jl's, written as LibreOffice writes them (F-032).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setBorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setOutsideBorder","page":"API reference","text":"setOutsideBorder(sheet, ref; outside) -> -1\n\nThe border around a range as a whole: setBorder(sheet, ref; outside).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setOutsideBorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setAlignment","page":"API reference","text":"setAlignment(sheet, ref; horizontal, vertical, wrapText, shrink, indent, rotation) -> String or -1\n\nSet cell alignment (address forms as setFont): horizontal (\"left\", \"center\", \"right\", \"fill\", \"justify\", \"centerContinuous\", \"distributed\"), vertical (\"top\", \"center\", \"bottom\", \"justify\", \"distributed\"), wrapText, shrink, indent (levels of 0.286 cm) and rotation (−90 to 90 degrees).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setAlignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getFont","page":"API reference","text":"getFont(sheet, ref) -> CellFont\ngetFont(sheet, row, col) / getFont(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\")\n\nThe font of one cell, in XLSX.jl's shape (D-032): font holds \"b\", \"i\", \"strike\" (=> nothing when set), \"u\" (nothing for single, Dict(\"val\" => \"double\")), \"sz\", \"name\" and \"color\" (Dict(\"rgb\" => \"FFRRGGBB\")), and other ODF text properties under \"odf:…\" keys. The values are those the cell's style resolves to, through its parent styles and the document's defaults; an unformatted cell, even an empty one, reports the default font. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getFont"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getFill","page":"API reference","text":"getFill(sheet, ref) -> CellFill\n\nThe background of one cell: fill[\"patternFill\"] is Dict(\"patternType\" => \"solid\", \"fgrgb\" => \"FFRRGGBB\"), or Dict(\"patternType\" => \"none\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getFill"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getBorder","page":"API reference","text":"getBorder(sheet, ref) -> CellBorder\n\nThe borders of one cell: border[\"left\"] (and right, top, bottom) is Dict(\"style\" => …, \"rgb\" => \"FFRRGGBB\") or nothing; border[\"diagonal\"] also has \"direction\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getBorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getAlignment","page":"API reference","text":"getAlignment(sheet, ref) -> CellAlignment\n\nThe alignment of one cell: alignment[\"alignment\"] holds the attributes the cell's style sets, in XLSX.jl's names and values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getAlignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getDefaultFont","page":"API reference","text":"getDefaultFont(sheet) -> CellFont\n\nThe font of an unformatted cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getDefaultFont"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.CellFont","page":"API reference","text":"CellFont(fontId, font, applyFont)\n\nA cell's font, as getFont returns it (XLSX.jl's shape, D-032). fontId is the name of the cell's style (ODF has no font table).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.CellFont"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.CellFill","page":"API reference","text":"CellFill(fillId, fill, applyFill)\n\nA cell's fill, as getFill returns it (XLSX.jl's shape, D-032). fillId is the name of the cell's style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.CellFill"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.CellBorder","page":"API reference","text":"CellBorder(borderId, border, applyBorder)\n\nA cell's borders, as getBorder returns them (XLSX.jl's shape, D-032). borderId is the name of the cell's style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.CellBorder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.CellAlignment","page":"API reference","text":"CellAlignment(alignment, applyAlignment)\n\nA cell's alignment, as getAlignment returns it (XLSX.jl's shape, D-032): alignment[\"alignment\"] holds XLSX.jl's attributes (horizontal, vertical, wrapText, shrinkToFit, indent, textRotation) that the cell's style sets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.CellAlignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setFormat","page":"API reference","text":"setFormat(sheet, ref; format) -> String or -1\nsetFormat(sheet, row, col; format)\nsetFormat(xf, \"Sheet1.A1:B2\"; format)\n\nSet the number format of cells (address forms as setFont). format is:\n\na built-in name: \"General\", \"Number\" (0.00), \"Currency\", \"Percentage\" (0%), \"ShortDate\" (m/d/yyyy), \"LongDate\" (d-mmm-yy), \"Time\" (h:mm:ss), \"Scientific\" (##0.0E+0);\na built-in id, as a string (\"39\" is #,##0.00_);(#,##0.00));\nor a format code, checked as Excel checks it (\"#,##0.00\", \"d mmm yyyy\", \"[Red]0.0%\").\n\nThe code is stored as an ODF data style, in the form LibreOffice writes for it (F-031): a code of several sections becomes one style per section, chosen by style:map conditions. A value whose type doesn't fit the format is not converted: a date cell given \"0.00\" stays a date cell, which LibreOffice shows as a number and retypes when it saves (F-011). Returns the name of the cell's new style for a single cell, else -1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setFormat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getFormat","page":"API reference","text":"getFormat(sheet, ref) -> CellFormat\ngetFormat(sheet, row, col) / getFormat(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\")\n\nThe number format of one cell: its code (read back from the ODF data style, as Excel's code for it) and an id, Excel's built-in id where the code is a built-in one, else one from 164 up. An unformatted cell has General (id 0). Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ODSFiles.CellFormat","page":"API reference","text":"CellFormat(numFmtId, format, applyNumberFormat)\n\nA cell's number format, as getFormat returns it (XLSX.jl's shape, D-032): format[\"numFmt\"] holds \"numFmtId\" and \"formatCode\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.CellFormat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getText","page":"API reference","text":"getText(sheet, ref) -> String\ngetText(sheet, row, col) / getText(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\")\n\nThe text a cell shows: its value in its number format, as LibreOffice shows it when the column is wide enough (F-030). 1234.5 formatted \"#,##0.00\" is \"1,234.50\", a date formatted \"d mmm yyyy\" is \"7 Mar 2026\". A formula shows its stored value; an empty cell, or a formula without a value, is \"\"; an error shows its text.\n\nLibreOffice differs from Excel in places, and getText follows LibreOffice: General shows up to 15 significant digits, ? pads with a figure space (U+2007), 0.## of a whole number has no point, seconds are truncated, and day numbers count from the document's null date. A fill (*x) is left out, as there is no column width. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getText"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformFont","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformFont(sheet, ref; kw...) -> String\n\nFormat the first (top-left) cell of ref with setFont's keywords, then give every cell of ref that cell's font. Returns the first cell's style name. setUniformFill, setUniformBorder and setUniformAlignment do the same for fills, borders and alignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformFont"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformFill","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformFill(sheet, ref; kw...) -> String\n\nSee setUniformFont.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformFill"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformBorder","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformBorder(sheet, ref; kw...) -> String\n\nSee setUniformFont. outside can't be used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformBorder"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformAlignment","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformAlignment(sheet, ref; kw...) -> String\n\nSee setUniformFont.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformAlignment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformFormat","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformFormat(sheet, ref; format) -> String\n\nFormat the first (top-left) cell of ref with setFormat, then give every cell of ref that cell's number format. Returns the first cell's style name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformFormat"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setUniformStyle","page":"API reference","text":"setUniformStyle(sheet, ref) -> String\n\nGive every cell of ref the style of its first (top-left) cell. Returns that style's name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setUniformStyle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setColumnWidth","page":"API reference","text":"setColumnWidth(sheet, ref; width, unit=:chars) -> 0\nsetColumnWidth(sheet, row, col; width, unit=:chars)\nsetColumnWidth(sheet, col; width) / setColumnWidth(xf, \"Sheet1.B:D\"; width)\n\nSet the width of the columns ref names (a cell, range, column range, defined name, or rows and columns as integers, ranges, vectors or :; only the columns are used).\n\nwidth is in XLSX.jl's character units by default: it is stored as LibreOffice stores the width of a file XLSX.jl wrote with the same width (0.2152 cm × (width + 0.7109375), D-034). With unit = :cm, :mm, :in or :pt, width is a length. A width may be 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setColumnWidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getColumnWidth","page":"API reference","text":"getColumnWidth(sheet, ref; unit=:chars) -> Union{Nothing, Float64}\ngetColumnWidth(sheet, row, col; unit=:chars)\n\nThe width of the column of one cell, or nothing if its column style gives none. In characters by default, as XLSX.jl's getter gives them (the width set, plus 0.7109375); with unit, as a length. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getColumnWidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setRowHeight","page":"API reference","text":"setRowHeight(sheet, ref; height) -> 0\nsetRowHeight(sheet, row, col; height) / setRowHeight(sheet, row; height)\n\nSet the height, in points, of the rows ref names (only the rows are used). LibreOffice then keeps the height rather than fitting it to the text (use-optimal-row-height=\"false\"). A height may be 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setRowHeight"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getRowHeight","page":"API reference","text":"getRowHeight(sheet, ref) -> Union{Nothing, Float64}\ngetRowHeight(sheet, row, col)\n\nThe height of the row of one cell, as XLSX.jl's getter gives it: in points, plus 0.2109375. nothing if its row style gives no height, -1 if the sheet has no such row. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getRowHeight"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.autofitColumns","page":"API reference","text":"autofitColumns(sheet, [cols=:]; min_width=0, max_width=255) -> Dict{Int,Float64}\n\nSet the width of columns to fit the text their cells show (getText), as LibreOffice's optimal column width does: the widest text, in each cell's font, size and boldness, plus 7.9 pt (F-034). cols is : (every column with something in it), a column number, a range or vector of numbers, or letters such as \"B\", \"B:D\" or \"A,C:E\".\n\nWidths are in characters, as setColumnWidth takes them, kept between min_width and max_width. A column whose cells show nothing keeps its width; cells in merged ranges are left out. Returns the width set for each column, by column number.\n\nThe widths are approximate: characters are measured with the widths Excel uses for Calibri, Aptos, Arial, Times New Roman and Courier New, LibreOffice's Liberation fonts as their metric twins (Arial, Times New Roman, Courier New), and other fonts as Calibri.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.autofitColumns"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.setCellStyle","page":"API reference","text":"setCellStyle(sheet, ref, name) -> String or -1\nsetCellStyle(sheet, row, col, name)\nsetCellStyle(xf, \"Sheet1.A1:B2\", name)\n\nApply the named cell style name to cells (address forms as setFont). name is the name LibreOffice shows (\"Heading 1\") or the style's name in the file (Heading_20_1). LibreOffice's built-in styles (Heading, Heading 1, Heading 2, Text, Note, Footnote, Hyperlink, Status, Good, Neutral, Bad, Warning, Error, Accent, Accent 1 to 3, Result) are added to a file that lacks them.\n\nAs in LibreOffice, a cell's direct formatting stays, except for what the style sets: a style with a font size replaces the cell's own size, and a cell's number format stays unless the style has one (so dates stay dates). Returns the name of the cell's new style for a single cell (name's style name when no direct formatting is left), else -1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.setCellStyle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getCellStyle","page":"API reference","text":"getCellStyle(sheet, ref) -> String\ngetCellStyle(sheet, row, col)\ngetCellStyle(xf, \"Sheet1.A1\")\n\nThe named cell style of a cell, by the name LibreOffice shows (\"Heading 1\", \"Good\", \"Default\"). A cell with direct formatting reports the named style under it. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getCellStyle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.addCellStyle!","page":"API reference","text":"addCellStyle!(xf, name; parent=\"Default\", font, fill, border, alignment, format) -> String\n\nDefine the named cell style name in the file, to be applied with setCellStyle. Its properties are given as the setters take them:\n\nfont: a NamedTuple of setFont's keywords, such as (bold = true, size = 14)\nfill: setFill's, such as (pattern = \"solid\", fgColor = \"FFFF00\")\nborder: setBorder's, such as (allsides = [\"style\" => \"thin\"],)\nalignment: setAlignment's, such as (horizontal = \"center\",)\nformat: a number format, as setFormat takes it\n\nProperties not given come from parent, a named style (by default Default; LibreOffice's built-in styles are added when needed). The style is written to styles.xml with the name encoded as LibreOffice encodes it (\"My Style\" is My_20_Style, shown as \"My Style\"). Returns that name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.addCellStyle!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.mergeCells","page":"API reference","text":"mergeCells(sheet, ref) -> 0\nmergeCells(sheet, row, col) / mergeCells(xf, \"Sheet1.A1:C2\")\n\nMerge the cells of a range. The merged cell shows the value of the range's first (top-left) cell; the other cells are emptied, as XLSX.jl and Excel do, and become covered cells (table:covered-table-cell). Their formatting is kept. A list of ranges merges each range; whole columns or rows are taken to the used area, as in XLSX.jl. A range may not be a single cell, nor overlap a merged range. Unlike XLSX.jl, a range may reach past the used area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.mergeCells"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.removeMergedCells","page":"API reference","text":"removeMergedCells(sheet, ref) -> 0\nremoveMergedCells(sheet) / removeMergedCells(sheet, :)\n\nUnmerge the merged range that encloses ref (so naming any one of its cells is enough), or every merged range of the sheet. Values are not changed. A range no merge encloses is left alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.removeMergedCells"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getMergedCells","page":"API reference","text":"getMergedCells(sheet) -> Union{Vector{CellRange}, Nothing}\n\nThe merged ranges of a sheet, in reading order, or nothing if it has none. Works on read-only files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getMergedCells"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.isMergedCell","page":"API reference","text":"isMergedCell(sheet, ref; mergedCells=missing) -> Bool\n\nWhether a cell is part of a merged range. mergedCells (from getMergedCells) spares finding the merges again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.isMergedCell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ODSFiles.getMergedBaseCell","page":"API reference","text":"getMergedBaseCell(sheet, ref; mergedCells=missing) -> Union{Nothing, NamedTuple}\n\nThe base (top-left) cell of the merged range holding ref, and its value: (baseCell = CellRef, baseValue = value), or nothing if the cell is not merged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.getMergedBaseCell"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ODSFiles.SheetCore","page":"API reference","text":"SheetCore\n\nThe format-independent core shared by the spreadsheet back ends (D-001): cell references and ranges, tabular data and Tables.jl input, number formats and displayed text, date serial numbers, text widths for autofit, colours, and reading zip parts.\n\nIt knows nothing about ODF or OOXML. It is developed inside ODSFiles.jl, and is kept self-contained so that it can later become a package of its own that XLSX.jl can also use (see records/core-migration.md in the workspace). Most of its code is adapted from XLSX.jl 0.13.4 (MIT License, Copyright (c) 2018-2026 Felipe Noronha Tavares and other contributors); each file says what it came from and what changed.\n\nIts names are the ones XLSX.jl uses, so that adopting it is a matter of imports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ODSFiles.SheetCore"},{"category":"section","location":"xlsx/#Coming-from-XLSX.jl","page":"Coming from XLSX.jl","text":"ODSFiles follows XLSX.jl's interface, so most code needs only its file names changed:\n\nXLSX.jl ODSFiles\nXLSX.readxlsx, XLSX.openxlsx readods, openods\nXLSX.newxlsx, XLSX.writexlsx, XLSX.savexlsx newods, writeods, saveods\nXLSX.streamxlsx streamods\nXLSXFile, XLSXError ODSFile, ODSError\n\nThese work as in XLSX.jl: readtable, gettable, eachtablerow, readdata, getdata, indexing, getcell, getcellrange, eachrow, iserror, geterror, setdata!, writetable, writetable!, addsheet!, writerow!, writerows! and the Tables.jl interface.\n\nThe formatting functions work as in XLSX.jl too, with the differences listed in Formatting:\n\nsetFont, setFill, setBorder, setAlignment, setFormat and their getters\nthe setUniform* variants and setOutsideBorder\ngetText\nmergeCells and the other merge functions\nsetColumnWidth, setRowHeight, their getters and autofitColumns\nwritetable's column_widths and column_formats","title":"Coming from XLSX.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"xlsx/#Differences","page":"Coming from XLSX.jl","text":"References print in LibreOffice's notation (Sheet1.A1); Excel's (Sheet1!A1) is still accepted. Lists of areas can also use ~.\nFormulas read and write in LibreOffice's notation (=SUM(Sheet2.A1:B2,1)), not Excel's.\nDurations: a time cell of 24 hours or more, or a negative one, reads as a Dates.CompoundPeriod, not a Time.\nCells: a Cell holds its decoded value, its value type as a symbol, its style's name and its formula text, rather than XLSX.jl's internal indices.\nError codes are the text LibreOffice shows, including its own Err:NNN.\nSaving an edited file keeps everything not edited, rather than rewriting the file.\ndisplay_text=false (no XLSX.jl counterpart) writes smaller files; see Saving and writing.\nFormatting works on ODF's styles: setters return a style name, getters work on read-only files, and empty cells and whole columns are formatted as LibreOffice formats them. ODSFiles also has named cell styles (setCellStyle, addCellStyle!), which XLSX.jl lacks. See Formatting.\nInserting and deleting rows and columns (insertRows, insertColumns, deleteRows, deleteColumns) take XLSX.jl's arguments and behave as LibreOffice does: a deleted reference is stored as #REF! without its sheet, 3-D references follow, new rows copy the formatting of the row above (borders included), and new columns that of the column to their left, with the width of the column they push right.\nNot yet available: other sheet operations (renaming, copying, moving, deleting sheets), Excel-style tables, conditional formats, comments, images and charts, and writetable's as_table and freeze_header.","title":"Differences"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/formulas/#Formulas","page":"Formulas","text":"OpenDocument stores formulas in OpenFormula syntax (of:=SUM([.A1:.B2];1)). ODSFiles reads and writes them in the notation LibreOffice shows in its formula bar:\n\ngetFormula(sh, \"C1\")                 # \"=SUM(A1:B2,1)\"\ngetFormula(sh, \"C1\"; raw = true)     # \"of:=SUM([.A1:.B2];1)\"\n\nsetFormula(sh, \"D1\"; val = \"=AVERAGE(A1:A10,'Other sheet'.B2)\")\nsetFormula(sh, \"D2\"; val = \"of:=[.A1]*2\", raw = true)\n\nThe notation is LibreOffice's with English function names and separators: , between arguments, and in inline arrays , between columns and ; between rows ({1,2;3,4}). Function names are translated with LibreOffice's own table, so IFS is stored as COM.MICROSOFT.IFS, CEILING.XCL as COM.MICROSOFT.CEILING, and so on.\n\nODSFiles does not calculate formulas. A formula written by ODSFiles has no result until the file is opened in LibreOffice (or another spreadsheet program), which computes it.","title":"Formulas"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/values/#Cell-values-and-types","page":"Cell values and types","text":"An OpenDocument cell stores its value with a type. ODSFiles reads it as follows:\n\nCell Julia value\nnumber, percentage, currency Int64 if the value is whole and its number format shows no decimals, else Float64\ndate Date, or DateTime if it has a time or its format shows one\ntime (a duration) Dates.Time from 0 to under 24 hours; otherwise a Dates.CompoundPeriod such as 1 day, 12 hours\nboolean Bool\ntext String (several paragraphs are joined with \"\\n\")\nempty, empty text, error missing\n\nPercentages are read as their value (50% reads as 0.5). The currency of a currency cell is in its Cell (getcell(sheet, \"B2\").currency).","title":"Cell values and types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/values/#Errors","page":"Cell values and types","text":"A formula whose result is an error reads as missing, as in XLSX.jl. iserror and geterror tell them apart, with the text LibreOffice shows:\n\niserror(sh, \"C1\")      # true\ngeterror(sh, \"C1\")     # \"#DIV/0!\", or LibreOffice's own codes such as \"Err:502\"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/values/#Cells","page":"Cell values and types","text":"getcell gives the whole cell: its value type, value, formula, style name, currency, error text and merge spans.\n\nc = getcell(sh, \"B2\")\nc.datatype     # :float, :percentage, :currency, :date, :time, :boolean, :string, :error, :empty\nc.value\nc.style        # the name of its cell style\n\nWhere a sheet holds nothing, getcell returns an EmptyCell.","title":"Cells"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/values/#Writing-values","page":"Cell values and types","text":"Writing takes numbers, strings, Bool, Date, DateTime, Time, periods (durations) and missing or nothing (an empty cell). Whole numbers keep their type (7 reads back as 7, 7.0 as 7.0), and floats are written exactly.\n\nDates, times and booleans need a number format, or LibreOffice treats them as plain numbers the next time it saves. ODSFiles gives them ISO formats (2026-11-18, 2026-11-18 13:45:00, [HH]:MM:SS, TRUE). A cell whose existing format fits the new value keeps it.","title":"Writing values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Saving-and-writing","page":"Saving and writing","text":"","title":"Saving and writing"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Modes","page":"Saving and writing","text":" \nreadods(path), openods(path) read only\nopenods(path; mode=\"rw\") read and edit; save with saveods or writeods\nopenods(path; mode=\"w\"), newods a new, empty file\n\nWith the do-block form of openods, a file opened for writing is saved when the block ends (not if it throws).","title":"Modes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Editing-keeps-the-rest-of-the-file","page":"Saving and writing","text":"ODSFiles does not rebuild a file it edits. It copies the original and rewrites only the rows that hold edited or formatted cells, and the columns whose formats changed:\n\nuntouched sheets, settings, images, charts and the other parts stay byte for byte\nstyles are only added: new automatic styles go into content.xml, and named styles into styles.xml (Formatting). Styles ODSFiles made that no cell uses any more are not written.\nin an edited row, the cells you did not change keep their formatting, comments and links\n\nA file saved without edits is identical to the original.","title":"Editing keeps the rest of the file"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Formats-and-versions","page":"Saving and writing","text":"writeods chooses the format from the file name: .ods, .ots (a template) or .fods (flat XML, one file without zip). New files are written in ODF 1.4, the version LibreOffice writes, or in ODF 1.3 with version=\"1.3\". An edited file keeps its version.","title":"Formats and versions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Smaller-files:-display_textfalse","page":"Saving and writing","text":"Like LibreOffice, ODSFiles writes each number, date, time and boolean cell twice: as its value and as the text shown in the cell. Spreadsheet programs recompute the shown text from the value, so it can be left out:\n\nwritetable(\"big.ods\", df; display_text = false)\nwriteods(\"out.ods\", xf; display_text = false)\nstreamods(f, \"big.ods\"; display_text = false)\n\nFor numeric data the files are then about half the size and faster to write. Tools that read only the shown text would see such cells as empty.","title":"Smaller files: display_text=false"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving/#Large-files","page":"Saving and writing","text":"streamods and writetable(...; streaming=true) write rows as they are produced, in bounded memory.\nreadtable and readdata read a file only as far as the sheet they need.\nopenods(path; enable_cache=false) with eachrow reads rows without keeping them.","title":"Large files"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Reading","page":"Getting started","text":"readods opens a file and returns an ODSFile. A sheet is read the first time it is used.\n\nusing ODSFiles\n\nxf = readods(\"data.ods\")\nsheetnames(xf)            # the sheets, in order\nsh = xf[\"Sheet1\"]         # or xf[1]\n\nsh[\"A1\"]                  # a value: a number, string, Date, ..., or missing\nsh[\"A1:C3\"]               # a Matrix{Any}\nsh[2, 3]                  # row 2, column 3\nsh[:, 1]                  # a whole column, as far as the sheet has data\nsh[:]                     # everything\n\nTo read one value or range without keeping the file open, use readdata:\n\nreaddata(\"data.ods\", \"Sheet1\", \"B2:D5\")\nreaddata(\"data.ods\", \"Sheet1.B2:D5\")       # LibreOffice's notation\nreaddata(\"data.ods\", \"Sheet1!B2:D5\")       # Excel's notation works too","title":"Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Tables","page":"Getting started","text":"A table is a block of rows with a header. readtable reads it into a DataTable, which any Tables.jl sink accepts:\n\nusing DataFrames\ndf = DataFrame(readtable(\"data.ods\", \"Sheet1\"))\n\nWithout further keywords, the table starts at the first row with data and spans the first run of non-empty header cells. Columns, the first row, labels and the handling of empty rows can all be chosen; see readtable.","title":"Tables"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Rows","page":"Getting started","text":"eachrow gives the rows that have content:\n\nfor r in eachrow(xf[\"Sheet1\"])\n    println(row_number(r), \": \", r[1], \" \", r[\"B\"])\nend\n\nFor files too large to hold in memory, open them with enable_cache=false: rows are then read as they are iterated.\n\nopenods(\"big.ods\"; enable_cache = false) do xf\n    for r in eachrow(xf[1])\n        # ...\n    end\nend","title":"Rows"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Writing-a-new-file","page":"Getting started","text":"using Dates\n\nxf = newods(\"Results\")\nsh = xf[\"Results\"]\nsh[\"A1\"] = \"Name\"\nsh[\"B1\"] = \"Score\"\nsh[\"A2:B3\"] = [\"Ann\" 9.5; \"Bob\" 7]\nsh[\"C2\"] = Date(2026, 11, 18)\nsetFormula(sh, \"B4\"; val = \"=SUM(B2:B3)\")\nwriteods(\"results.ods\", xf)\n\nOr write a whole table at once:\n\nwritetable(\"results.ods\", df; sheetname = \"Results\")\nwritetable(\"all.ods\", \"Results\" => df, \"Notes\" => notes)","title":"Writing a new file"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Formatting","page":"Getting started","text":"XLSX.jl's formatting functions work on ODS cells, and so do LibreOffice's named styles:\n\nsetFont(sh, \"A1:B1\"; bold = true)\nsetFill(sh, \"A1:B1\"; pattern = \"solid\", fgColor = \"DDEEFF\")\nsetFormat(sh, \"B2:B4\"; format = \"0.0\")\nsetColumnWidth(sh, \"A\"; width = 15)\nmergeCells(sh, \"A6:B6\")\nsetCellStyle(sh, \"A4\", \"Accent 1\")\n\nSee Formatting for the rest.","title":"Formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Editing-a-file","page":"Getting started","text":"Open it with mode=\"rw\". The do-block form saves the file when the block ends:\n\nopenods(\"budget.ods\"; mode = \"rw\") do xf\n    xf[\"Costs\"][\"B7\"] = 1250.0\n    addsheet!(xf, \"Summary\")[\"A1\"] = \"Total\"\nend\n\nEverything ODSFiles does not change — styles, column widths, comments, images, charts, settings — is kept exactly as it was (Saving and writing).","title":"Editing a file"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Streaming-a-large-file","page":"Getting started","text":"streamods writes rows as they are produced, in bounded memory:\n\nstreamods(\"big.ods\") do s\n    sh = addsheet!(s, \"data\"; columns = [\"n\", \"square\"])\n    for n in 1:1_000_000\n        writerow!(sh, (n, n^2))\n    end\nend","title":"Streaming a large file"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/references/#References-and-names","page":"References and names","text":"","title":"References and names"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/references/#Cells-and-ranges","page":"References and names","text":"Within a sheet, references are the usual ones: \"B2\", \"A1:C3\", whole columns \"A:C\", whole rows \"2:4\". $ marks are accepted (\"$A$1\"). Several areas are joined with , or LibreOffice's ~:\n\nsh[\"A1:A4,C1\"]     # a vector of matrices, one per area\nsh[\"A1:A4~C1\"]     # the same\n\nRows and columns can also be given as numbers, ranges or vectors: sh[2, 3], sh[2:5, [1, 3]], sh[:, 2].","title":"Cells and ranges"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/references/#Naming-a-sheet","page":"References and names","text":"ODSFiles reads and prints sheet-qualified references in LibreOffice's notation, and also accepts Excel's:\n\nLibreOffice Excel \nSheet1.A1 Sheet1!A1 \n'My sheet'.A1:B2 'My sheet'!A1:B2 quoted names\n$Sheet1.$A$1  absolute\nSheet1.A1~Sheet1.C3 Sheet1!A1,Sheet1!C3 lists\n\nxf[\"Sheet1.B2\"]            # a value from the file\nxf[\"'My sheet'!A1:C3\"]\n\nReferences that ODSFiles returns, such as SheetCellRef, print in LibreOffice's notation.","title":"Naming a sheet"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/references/#Defined-names","page":"References and names","text":"Named ranges and named expressions, for the whole file or for one sheet, can be used wherever a reference can:\n\nxf[\"Totals\"]              # a workbook name\nsh[\"LocalRange\"]          # a name local to the sheet\n\nA name whose expression is a constant gives that value. A name that is a formula can't be read: ODSFiles does not evaluate formulas.\n\nSheet and defined names match without regard to case, as in LibreOffice.","title":"Defined names"},{"category":"section","location":"#ODSFiles.jl","page":"Home","text":"Read and write OpenDocument spreadsheets (.ods, .fods, .ots) in pure Julia.\n\nOpenDocument (ISO/IEC 26300) is the spreadsheet format of LibreOffice and of the public bodies that standardise on open formats. ODSFiles reads and writes it directly: no Python, no LibreOffice and no other program is needed.\n\nIts interface follows XLSX.jl, so code written for Excel files carries over with few changes (see Coming from XLSX.jl). Where LibreOffice works differently, ODSFiles follows LibreOffice: references print as Sheet1.A1, formulas read and write in LibreOffice's notation, and sheets are as LibreOffice saves them.","title":"ODSFiles.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/TimG1964/ODSFiles.jl\")\n\nODSFiles supports Julia 1.10 and later.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-look","page":"Home","text":"using ODSFiles\n\nxf = readods(\"budget.ods\")        # open a file\nsheetnames(xf)                    # [\"Income\", \"Costs\"]\nsh = xf[\"Costs\"]\nsh[\"B3\"]                          # one value\nsh[\"A1:C10\"]                      # a matrix of values\n\ndt = readtable(\"budget.ods\", \"Costs\")   # a table, for DataFrames or any Tables.jl sink\n\nwritetable(\"out.ods\", dt; sheetname = \"Costs\")   # write it back","title":"A first look"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-does","page":"Home","text":"Reading: typed values (numbers, text, dates, times and durations, booleans, errors), repeated rows and cells, merged cells, named ranges, formulas, hidden sheets; reading a whole file, one sheet, or rows as they stream.\nTables: readtable, gettable, eachtablerow and the Tables.jl interface.\nWriting: new files, editing an existing file, tables, and streaming large files row by row. An edited file keeps everything ODSFiles does not change exactly as it was.\nFormats: .ods, flat .fods and .ots templates; ODF 1.4 (as LibreOffice writes) or 1.3.\nFormatting: fonts, fills, borders, alignment, number formats (shown as LibreOffice shows them), named cell styles, merged cells, column widths, row heights and autofit, with XLSX.jl's functions.\nSpeed: comparable with XLSX.jl on XLSX.jl's own benchmarks.\n\nSheet operations, conditional formats and rich objects (comments, images, charts) are planned.","title":"What it does"}]
}
